WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.620
Film Courage: Can you recall the meeting you
had with Ridley Scott?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.630
Corey Mandell, Screenwriter/Instructor: Like
it was yesterday!

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.460
And I will answer that but I’m going to
be annoying and answering something…I want

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.670
to talk about the meeting I had before Ridley
Scott because I think there is a teaching

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.840
moment in here.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:23.289
So I had an idea for a movie and I pitched
it to my agent and she goes “Oh!

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.340
I think this is great.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.439
Let’s take it out to the marketplace.”

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:30.529
And then she said “Let’s make a list of
our dream places that we want to sell it to.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:33.390
So if you could sell it to anyone, who would
it be?”

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.890
And it was so heady I thought “Oh yeah,
who would I sell it to?”

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:37.890
And I said “Ridley Scott!”

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.059
And she kind of laughed like “Okay…yeah.

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:41.610
We will take it there and then when they pass
we will go down the list and then we will

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.010
go to places that actually could.”

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.940
So I go into Ridley Scott’s and I pitch
with Sue Williams, who is a development executive

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.250
(smart, nicest woman).

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.030
And this is really important when you are
pitching is you are trying to get as much

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.250
information.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.740
So I was pitching her this project and at
some point I saw her body language shift and

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.149
I go “Oooh, is something off about that?”

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:07.890
And she goes “Ridley doesn’t like these
kind of things.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.580
I think this is really exciting for him but
not if it is going down that road.”

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.940
And I’m like “What kind of things does
Ridley Scott like?”

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.850
And so she started sharing things and it was
so helpful so we could kind of retool the

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:18.850
pitch together.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.009
And so she was instrumental.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:24.979
When you go to someone you are pitching you
are trying to get as much information from

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:26.049
that person.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:30.020
And sometimes writers have ego and they’re
like “Yeah I’m going in and I’m pitching

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.140
to the lowest person on the totem pole.”

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.689
That’s great because you get information
about the decision maker.

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:41.920
So Sue Williams helped me reformulate the
pitch in a way that would be a better fit

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.920
for Ridley Scott.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.680
So that was amazing and I will be forever
grateful for her.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:53.259
Then I got brought in to pitch to Mimi who
at the time was Ridley’s producing partner

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:55.920
and this is a long pitch.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.010
This is almost an hour pitch (a 50-minute
pitch).

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.659
This is not a short pitch.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:04.060
I always hate when people say “Your pitches
have to be this amount of time.”

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.370
There are no rules.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.399
Like first of all when someone says you have
to do your pitch in 10 minutes people think

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:10.300
that people will listen to you for 10 minutes.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.569
They will listen to you for as long as they’re
interested.

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:16.010
And if they’re not interested in you for
30-seconds, they will tune you out.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.730
And most pitches that sell are longer pitches
because people want to have the confidence

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.970
that this has been worked out and what they
are buying is legit.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.819
Anyways…it’s a long pitch.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:30.280
So I’m pitching to Mimi and it can’t be
going worse.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:35.870
She is sitting back like this, she’s not
making eye contact, she looks like she is

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:36.870
in pain.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:42.120
This pitch is so bad that it’s making her
go crazy.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:47.260
And the only reason I have a career is at
the time I wanted to be doing stand-up or

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.330
improv so I was pursuing that.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:53.730
And in improv I was learning that one of my
problems was (my weaknesses) when I thought

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.530
things were going well I was alive and animated.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.560
But when I thought things weren’t going
well, when I didn’t think the audience was

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.290
into it, my fear of rejection runs through
this, I would sort of back out and tune out

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.050
of the scene and kind of like “You guys
take it.”

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.830
I would just sort of drop out of the scene
which is you don’t do that when improvising.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:12.670
So what I was being taught was when you think
things are going terrible walk to the center

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:17.150
of the stage, take focus, say something and
just stay in there, stay alive.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:22.970
Don’t worry about peoples’ judgments,
follow your process.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:27.680
So I’m pitching and after about 7 minutes
I’m like “She hates me, she hates this

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.519
pitch, she’s never going to buy it, she’s
in pain.”

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:33.650
And that voice says “Do the short version
and get out of here.”

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.810
This is painful for her, this was painful
for you.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.480
Ridley was never going to buy this pitch.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.790
Just get out of here, wrap it up and go (which
I would do as a stand-up comic, terrible habit).

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:48.400
But another voice would go “Okay, you’re
never going to be a writer as evidenced by

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.740
the pain you are causing this woman.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.010
Maybe you can be a performer.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.220
So use this as a way of practicing your improv
stills.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.220
Stay engaged!

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.220
Stay Engaged!”

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.220
That voice went through my head.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:05.060
I stay engaged and I just did the pitch with
focus and gusto and try not to look too much

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.870
at Mimi and see how much pain she is.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.470
And then there was just silence, painful silence.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:17.870
And then she stands up and goes, “I don’t
get it.”

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.320
And then Sue Williams said “Well what don’t
you get?”

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.000
And she goes “All of it.”

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.380
Then she looked at me and said something like
“Maybe we will be in touch.”

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.380
And walked out.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.380
Sue didn’t know what to say.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.570
I didn’t know what to say and I left and
I drove home and called my agent and was like

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:33.570
“You were right.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.060
Ridley is not going to buy this.”

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.690
Then Sue calls me and I pick up the phone
and she goes “Hey when you pitched with

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.110
me you were really good.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.300
But when you pitched with Mimi…”

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.169
And I know where this is going “You were
on fire!

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:49.660
Mimi loved it!”

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:56.590
Now there’s a lesson in this which is don’t
try to read the room because I’ve pitched

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:01.800
to heads of studios that laughed and loved
it because when someone is hearing a pitch

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.190
they are trying to decide “Am I going to
buy this?

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.240
Am I going to take this to my boss.”

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.520
That can be stressful.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.710
I pitched to a studio once where he knew right
away he wasn’t going to buy it because they

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.830
had a similar idea in development so he relaxed
and just had fun with me.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:16.250
We had a great time but he already knew he
wasn’t going to buy it.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:21.430
I found out that Mimi only takes one or two
pitches to Ridley and when she was hearing

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.080
this pitch she was like “Oh my gosh!

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.669
This is something Ridley would really love.”

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:34.110
And I can’t speak for her but maybe she
was nervous because maybe she was thinking

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.240
“Do I really want to put my career on the
line with this guy?”

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.990
I was a nobody, I was in film school.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.550
I was like 23 and green as they came.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:47.720
And I think that she hearing the pitch was
probably having an internal debate.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.680
Again I can’t speak for her but that is
my guess.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.861
So Sue was like “Yeah, I probably should
have told you that is sort of the way she

00:05:57.861 --> 00:05:58.900
listens.”

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.210
So don’t read people’s…I’ve been in
rooms where they seemed like they hated it

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.740
and sometimes they do, sometimes they don’t.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.080
Stay committed, have integrity, do the pitch.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.030
So to your question (sorry for the long wind-up),
I was told that Ridley wouldn’t be back

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.470
in town for a month.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.389
Mimi did have one big concern that she wanted
changed.

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:23.720
It was a hard creative thing to figure out
but I’m like “I have a month to figure

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.960
it out.”

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.390
A few days later I told you I got a phone
call.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.350
Here is what I didn’t tell you.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.990
I don’t drink much alcohol, I’m just a
lightweight.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.490
I’m the cheapest date ever.

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:41.229
And the night before I was at some party and
I drank more than I’ve ever drank in my

00:06:41.229 --> 00:06:42.229
life.

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:45.030
And it doesn’t take much to get me drunk.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:49.509
I think that morning might have been my first
ever hangover or it was the worst-ever hangover.

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:54.150
So when the phone rang I hadn’t showered,
I hadn’t shaven, I was hungover, it was

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:58.639
my agent and she said “Ridley is in town
and he would like to hear the pitch.

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:00.370
He has a slot in an hour.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.370
Can he hear the pitch?”

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:06.200
And it takes 45 minutes to get there to Beverly
Hills from where I was and so I said…and

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:10.870
this is not a good idea…I said “Oh that
is exciting.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.800
Can we bump it up until 2:00?”

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.580
And there was this long pause and I know my
agent was thinking “Why did I sign this

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:20.690
guy?”

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:27.389
And she said “So Corey, Ridley is in town,
he has a slot, he’d like to hear your pitch

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:28.850
in an hour.”

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:32.990
And at least then I was like “Of course,
I will be there in an hour.”

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:39.099
It kind of works out to my favor, so I quickly
shave and shower and drink coffee and on the

00:07:39.099 --> 00:07:42.870
drive over I am figuring out how to solve
that creative problem.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:47.790
What happened was I got there with a second
to spare and they put me into the room with

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:48.790
him.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.430
I never had the chance to get nervous.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.470
I never had the chance to psych myself out.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.910
I was just on pure adrenaline.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.390
Ridley was the sweetest person ever.

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:03.300
He came into the room and I started to like
“It’s an honor…”

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.300
And it was!

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:12.949
BLADERUNNER, ALIEN, like it was a literal
honor to be in a room with him, let alone

00:08:12.949 --> 00:08:17.410
that he was going to listen to me pitch a
project and he just immediately said like

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.400
“We are just two creative people.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.800
I want to hear your idea.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.370
You don’t have to be great at performing.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.370
My staff loves it.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:25.370
I just want to hear…”

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:32.969
He went out of his way to make me feel comfortable
and that I belonged there.

00:08:32.969 --> 00:08:36.910
Because I did not feel like I belonged in
a room pitching to Ridley Scott.

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:43.089
And I was just on adrenaline, no time to prep
or psych myself out and so I just started

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:44.089
talking.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:51.620
And he was just…he listened and he nodded
and he was so receptive and he was so loving

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:56.519
and gentle, I’ll never forget that and he
was just amazing.

00:08:56.519 --> 00:09:04.500
Then at the end he asked the question that
you always want to hear which is (he said)

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.520
“So who else has heard this pitch?”

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.080
And I said “Nobody, you’re the first pitch.”

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:12.210
And he said “Well who else is going to hear
this pitch?”

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.930
And I gave some of the places that we had
meetings set up.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.240
And he looked at his staff and then he turned
to me and said “Who is your agent?”

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.930
And I told him (Dan at ICM).

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:25.370
He called my agent and said “I want to take
this off the market.”

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:32.190
So that was just like…I literally thought
I was dreaming!

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:37.950
Then I remember I was driving home in my cruddy
little car, my little Flintstones car (as

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:41.810
my wife called it) because you had to use
your feet to stop it (film school, no money

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:42.810
car).

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:47.600
And I was driving on the 405 and I started
shaking, I just was shaking.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.630
I had to pull over.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.820
And the thought that went through my head
wasn’t that I had just launched my career,

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.070
it wasn’t that I just sold it.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:10:00.190
The thought that went through my head was
“I just met Ridley Scott.”

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:06.590
I was so nerdy and not only that, once the
deal was consummated they said we want you

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:13.300
on the next plane to London so that Ridley
and his team will develop the structure with

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:17.959
you and they meant the literal “next plane
to London.”

00:10:17.959 --> 00:10:24.100
So my wife and I…my girlfriend at the time
(now wife Rebecca) she was like “I’ll

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.010
help you pack,” because I don’t know how
to pack.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.910
And she is getting all of my clothes and she
is like “Where is your suitcase?”

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.950
And I don’t have a suitcase.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:33.620
So we ran to Target and bought a suitcase
and everything.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.930
Then they flew me first class and I didn’t
know that first class existed.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:41.020
I didn’t know what first class was, I grew
up with no money.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:46.730
So the kept bringing me food and wine and
I was like “No, no, no.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:47.730
I can’t afford this.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.570
I don’t want to pay for all of this when
I land.”

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.990
And then at some point in London someone said
“No in first class it is all included.”

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:56.630
So on the way back I was like “More food!”

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:04.390
But Ridley treated me throughout the entire
process and he called me when they weren’t

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:07.550
going to make it and he explained why which
he didn’t have to do.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:15.070
Throughout the entire process he treated me
like a writer who belonged to be there and

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.610
someone who wanted to hear my opinion.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:22.140
He didn’t want me to just defer to him,
I mean it was amazing.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.470
I’ll forever be grateful for that.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:29.330
He spoiled me because I thought well that
is how everyone…I worked with some of the

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.470
best people in the business and that’s how
they treat writers and I’ve worked with

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.070
people who don’t treat writers that way.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.399
Film Courage: When you pulled over on the
405, do you remember was there a song playing

00:11:39.399 --> 00:11:40.490
on the radio?

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.860
Corey: I don’t…I just remember I was going
to crash.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:44.860
That is a good question.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:45.860
Gosh I don’t remember.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:50.470
I just kept thinking…I came home and my
girlfriend was like “So?”

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.390
And I’m like “I met Ridley Scott!”

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.420
And she’s like “I know you met Ridley
Scott.”

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.010
“Oh yeah…he bought it.”

00:11:56.010 --> 00:12:02.210
But I was just a little film school nerd excited
to meet Ridley Scott.

